   428	
   429	\begin{prp}\label{prp:fvsconnec}
   430		Let $H$ be the \fvig of a plane biconnected graph $G$ and let $S^\circ$ be a feedback vertex set in the dual $D$ of $G$. 
   431		Then $S^\circ$ induces a \cfc $S$ in $G$.
   432	\end{prp}
   433	
   434	\begin{proof}
   435	We need to show that $S^\circ$ is a face cover and that it is connected. 
   436	First, assume there is a vertex $v \in V(G)$ of degree $\deg(v) = d$ that is not incident to a vertex in $S^\circ$, i.e., a face of $G$.
   437	Since $G$ is biconnected, $v$ is incident to $d$ faces $f_1, \dots, f_d$, none of which is contained in $S^\circ$.
   438	But then $D[V(D) \setminus S^\circ]$ has a cycle $(f_1, \dots, f_d)$, a contradiction. 
   439	
   440	Next, we define $\overline{S^\circ} = V(D)\setminus S^\circ$ as the complement of the feedback vertex set $S^\circ$ in $D$. 

codex
Step 0 confirms the paper’s key equivalence is not merely “replay splits”: Sections 2 and 4 identify a split certificate with a connected face cover, and then with a feedback vertex set in the plane dual. The hard regime is specifically non-maximal biconnected plane graphs; maximal planar inputs are polynomial-time via subcubic feedback vertex set, so I’m excluding that easy regime. I’m now checking whether the paper-licensed cubic-planar Vertex Cover reduction can support a construction-known certificate without making the generated distribution easy.
